$DIR/main.p:7:2: cannot store (*N)(n) in package variable G, which outlives n
    (*N)(n) points into the object n refers to, which n keeps alive in f. n
    (declared at main.p:6) is an owned[N]: the object it refers to lives only
    while n holds it, and is dropped when n is dropped at the end of its scope
    in f, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the object,
    or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive n. When f
    returns, n is dropped and the object freed, but package variables live for
    the whole program, so package variable G would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store n itself, changing the type of G to match: an owned box moves there,
    and a shared or synced one is counted:
        G = n
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
